== The Hunt — How a Quest Actually Works

Every hunt runs the same loop. Master the loop and every monster in the game is a variation on it.

=== 1. Prepare

- *Eat dango.* The canteen meal grants stat boosts and a food skill — always eat before a hunt.
- *Check your loadout.* Carry potions, mega potions, a whetstone (melee), and quest-appropriate items (antidotes, nullberries). Save a standard loadout at the item box so preparation is one click.
- *Check the target.* The quest brief shows the monster; the Hunter's Notes (main menu) show its element/status weaknesses once you have researched it.
- *Bring your buddy.* Palamute and palico come along automatically once assigned.

=== 2. Find

When the quest starts you know the monster's area. Monster tracks — footprints, scales, scratches — raise your *tracking level* as you collect them, and the monster's icon appears on the map. If you lose it, gather more tracks, or ride your palamute and search its known area.

#tip[
  Set a subcamp as soon as you unlock one — it is a fast-travel point that saves minutes every hunt.
]

=== 3. Fight

The combat fundamentals are in *Combat Basics*. For the loop, what matters is *reading the monster*: it telegraphs every attack with an animation. Your job is to learn which animations mean what, and punish the openings after attacks whiff.

- *Part breaks* — break horns, tails, claws to stagger the monster and earn extra rewards.
- *Exhaustion and limping* — a tired monster drools and rests; a limping monster is near death (the skull icon appears on the minimap once your tracking is high enough).

=== 4. Finish — Kill or Capture

- *Kill:* when it is limping (or the skull icon shows), push the damage. On death, *carve* the body (usually three carves) for materials.
- *Capture:* place a trap (shock or pitfall) while it is weak, then hit it with two *tranq bombs*. Capture ends the hunt immediately and often grants more rewards — it is the efficient finisher.

#warn[
  A sleeping monster takes huge damage from the first hit. If it flees to sleep and you want the kill, place a bomb, wind up your biggest attack, and let it land — do not tickle it awake.
]

=== 5. Carting (Failing)

You have three *carts* per quest. Each time you faint, you are carried back to camp and lose one. Three faints — quest failed. Fainting is normal; the only mistake is not learning from it. After a faint you respawn at camp with reduced supplies, so restock from the item box before going back out.

=== 6. Rewards & Repeat

Quest rewards, part-break rewards, and carved/captured materials all flow back to the smithy. Every hunt either makes you stronger (gear), smarter (patterns), or both. That is the loop — and it is the entire game.

=== Gathering Along the Way

Never run past a node empty-handed:

- *Mining outcrops* give ores for weapons; *bone piles* give bones for armor.
- *Herbs, honey, and plants* refill your consumable crafting (honey is the road to mega potions — always grab it).
- *Endemic life* (spiribirds and more) gives temporary buffs; the colored birds you collect on the way to a monster are free stats. Collect them every hunt.
